---
layout: article
title: Usage monitors
description: Set thresholds on Appwrite usage metrics and monitor your whole project or an individual resource from the Console or a server SDK.
---

Usage monitors compare a metric with a threshold. In your project, open **Observe** > **Monitors** and click **Create monitor**. On Appwrite Cloud, choose **Usage** as the type. Under **Condition**, choose a **Metric**, what it **Applies to**, a comparator, and a threshold. **Applies to** only lists the scopes that metric supports.

# Choose a metric and scope {% #choose-a-metric-and-scope %}

| Scope | Example metrics |
| --- | --- |
| Project | Requests, outbound bandwidth, monthly active users, or storage used |
| Database | Database reads, database writes, or database storage |
| Bucket | Storage used |
| Function | Executions or execution GB-hours |
| Site | Executions or execution GB-hours |
| Dedicated database on Appwrite Cloud | CPU, memory, or connections |

Choose the whole project for an aggregate metric or select an individual resource when the metric supports it. The available metrics depend on the deployment and resources.

# Set the condition {% #set-the-condition %}

Under **Alert when the value is**, choose **greater than**, **greater than or equal**, **less than**, or **less than or equal**, and enter the threshold. A usage monitor compares one metric with one threshold.

Counters such as requests and outbound bandwidth are summed over the period in **Counted over**, from 5 minutes to 30 days. An outbound bandwidth monitor counted over 1 hour compares the sum over the preceding hour at each check. Gauges such as storage used compare the latest recorded value, so the Console hides **Counted over** for them.

{% only_dark %}
![Create monitor page with outbound bandwidth greater than 10 GB counted over 1 hour, above a chart of the last 24 hours of usage](/images/docs/monitors/dark/usage-condition.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Create monitor page with outbound bandwidth greater than 10 GB counted over 1 hour, above a chart of the last 24 hours of usage](/images/docs/monitors/usage-condition.avif)
{% /only_light %}

If a check has no usage reading, the monitor keeps its existing state. Check the monitor's last reading in the Console before adjusting the threshold.

On self-hosted Appwrite, usage monitors read the usage stats that the [`_APP_USAGE_STATS`](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/configuration/environment-variables) variable turns on. It's enabled by default. If you set it to `disabled`, Appwrite never checks your usage monitors.

# Create with a server SDK {% #create-with-a-server-sdk %}

Create an [API key](/docs/advanced/platform/api-keys) with the `monitors.write` scope, then call `createUsageMonitor`. This monitor opens an alert when the project sends more than 1 GB of outbound bandwidth in an hour, and notifies owners and developers at **Error** severity.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');

const monitors = new sdk.Monitors(client);

const monitor = await monitors.createUsageMonitor({
    monitorId: 'outbound-bandwidth',
    name: 'Outbound bandwidth spike',
    rules: [sdk.Query.greaterThan('network.outbound', 1000000000)],
    window: 3600, // optional
    cooldown: 1800, // optional
    notify: ['owner', 'developer'], // optional
    severity: sdk.MonitorSeverity.Error // optional
});
```

```server-python
from appwrite.client import Client
from appwrite.services.monitors import Monitors
from appwrite.query import Query
from appwrite.enums.monitor_severity import MonitorSeverity

client = Client()
client.set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
client.set_project('<PROJECT_ID>')
client.set_key('<API_KEY>')

monitors = Monitors(client)

monitor = monitors.create_usage_monitor(
    monitor_id = 'outbound-bandwidth',
    name = 'Outbound bandwidth spike',
    rules = [Query.greater_than('network.outbound', 1000000000)],
    window = 3600, # optional
    cooldown = 1800, # optional
    notify = ['owner', 'developer'], # optional
    severity = MonitorSeverity.ERROR # optional
)
```

